From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:16:54 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: Add the Glymur root complexes behind the USB4 host
 routers

Each USB4 host router of Glymur has a DesignWare root complex of its
own in the SoC, separate from the ones with a PHY. Its only link is the
PCIe tunnel that router carries, so it has no PHY and no PERST# pin:
the router's PCIe adapter stands in for the PHY, and PERST# is a
request the router passes down the tunnel. Nothing drives these
controllers, so the PCIe devices of USB4 and Thunderbolt docks and
drive enclosures never appear.

Add a driver for them. Such a root complex is described as a child of
its host router and is a device only while a tunnel exists; the router
driver creates and removes it. Probing therefore resets and configures
the controller, waits for the router to present the tunnel in the
slot, releases PERST# through the router, starts link training and
enumerates, and removal takes all of it down again. The router
provides two resets for this, the hold on its side of the pipe and
PERST#.

What is plugged into a USB4 port is not trusted. The driver does not
start unless the controller is mapped to an IOMMU and its root port is
described as external facing, which has the PCI core treat the devices
below as untrusted and the IOMMU core give them translated, strict
mappings.

The root port is a hot-plug slot whose card is the tunnel, as Windows
declares it: hot-plug capable and removable without notice. The other
slot features the controller announces by default (attention button,
power controller, latch, indicators, interlock) do not exist and are
cleared. The hot-plug handling of the port removes devices under the
rescan lock from the port's interrupt thread, and the removal of the
root bus takes that lock and waits for that thread, so the port is
unbound before the root bus is removed.

Across system suspend the devices behind the tunnel stay. The router
above this device is halted for the sleep and the link with it, so the
link is taken down in the noirq suspend callback, after the PCI core
has saved and powered down the devices, and the controller is started
again as at probe in the noirq resume callback, when the router has
restored the tunnel and before the PCI core restores the devices. If
the tunnel does not come back (an unplug during sleep) or the link
does not train, accesses below the root port complete without reaching
the link, the hot-plug handling of the port finds its device gone, and
the device is given up as below once the system is awake.

A link that goes down has the controller's outbound address space
blocked at once from the interrupt, so that an access to a device that
was just unplugged completes instead of faulting. The interrupt is in
place before the link is started and until the root bus is gone.

The controller does not train a link again once it went down, until it
is reset: after a hot reset from the root port the link stayed down
with the tunnel in place. Nothing may use the controller while it is
reset, and the Windows driver resets it only between hiding the root
bridge and announcing it again. So when the link is lost the driver
unbinds from the device, from a work item of its own, and the router
driver creates a new device if the tunnel still exists; the devices
come back as after a plug. For the same reason the root port is marked
as having no reset of the bus below it.

The bus bandwidth requested is the one in the platform's firmware
tables; the link speed the controller reports is of no use for it,
because the link is a tunnel.

pcie-qcom is left alone: it wants a PHY and a PERST# GPIO per port,
keeps its controllers for the life of the system, and has no remove
path.

The sequence and the register values are those of the platform's
Windows driver for these controllers. A few registers it writes are
not documented anywhere I could find and are named by offset.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig          |  12 +
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Makefile         |   1 +
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom-usb4.c | 849 ++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 862 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom-usb4.c

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
index dcfbe7e22..34fd41967 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
@@ -330,6 +330,18 @@ config PCIE_QCOM
 	  PCIe controller uses the DesignWare core plus Qualcomm-specific
 	  hardware wrappers.
 
+config PCIE_QCOM_USB4
+	bool "Qualcomm PCIe root complex behind a USB4 host router"
+	depends on OF && (ARCH_QCOM || COMPILE_TEST)
+	depends on PCI_MSI
+	select PCIE_DW_HOST
+	help
+	  Say Y here to support the PCIe root complexes of Qualcomm Glymur
+	  that carry the PCIe tunnels of its USB4 host routers. They are
+	  started and stopped by the USB4 host router driver as devices are
+	  plugged in, and are needed for the PCIe devices of USB4 and
+	  Thunderbolt docks, drive enclosures and graphics enclosures.
+
 config PCIE_QCOM_EP
 	tristate "Qualcomm PCIe controller (endpoint mode)"
 	depends on OF && (ARCH_QCOM || COMPILE_TEST)
